  adam gaffin,

  i saw your request on kidsnet about censoring porno from the nets. this
appears to be a problem because teachers and parents in the lower grades
conspire to prohibit sexual information to K-12 students, so once in
college (they escape the broadband censorship) there is a surge of juvenille
verbage about sex on the networks.  the subject material itself is really
very juvenille, something that would probably be exhausted in interest by
13 or 14 years of age if unrestricted.  repressing the discussion of
sexual matters until 19 or 20 years of age drives the internet mechanisms
you are worried about today.

  now i know this does not help at all for your problem of introducing
internet mechanisms into the local school systems.  here is how i handle
this in northern florida.  when people talk about 'the internet' there are
two levels of data exchange, and the censorhip mechanism works effectively
for the lower level (email) but is almost impossible at the advanced (ftp
and telnet).

  email is handled on a store-and-forward basis.  meaning that
messages are collected somewhere in the system and sent on to their
destination at another time.  email utilities usually have a local level
of storage before they are distributed out to LAN users.  at that point it
is easy to use textual utilities that will scan the files for selected
objectionable vocabulary.  the most common utility for doing this is
called 'grep' (generalized representation expression parser).  usenet is
conducted entirely in massive store-and-forward blocks, and they are
organized so the material your constituents will find objectionable are
in batches you need not subscribe to.

  ftp (file transfer protocol) and telnet are realtime synchronized use of
networked computers.  this is actually what most people think of when they
say they are using 'the internet'.  since these utilities are active while
the user is directly on the keyboard, there is no effective method of
censoring what the end user is doing with them.  the same sites which
contain the sexual material also contain material which teachers would
think as educationally valid.  there will be no way to censor this material
until after it is downloaded.  irc-chat is another realtime mechanism
available on the internet, where user type messages directly to each other.
this is a place where sexually-loaded flame wars frequently florish.

  i think the most common prohibition will be to prevent student use of
the realtime tcp/ip protocols.  this will delay the problem, since all the
ftp services can be requested through store-and-forward email.  in the
long run, the solution to the problem is to involve the students directly
in controlling their own network conduct.  we do not teach students how to
participate in reasonable democratic controls through censorship and
thought police.

  from experience i can tell you that flame wars not really a problem. it
is a sign of cyberadolescence which users pass through and outgrow.  even
stodgey professors emeritii will engage in this activity until they learn
how to express their feelings in virtual communication space.  we cannot
expect any different standards of school kids.  so when i see a flame war
erupting, i just monitor the activity and let the kids know that if they
feel their emotions are getting out of hand, i can apply the dampers.
this is something they need to request of me (as email postmaster), and so
far it has always been handled successfully by themselves.  haven't really
gotten to a good burn level though, the email volume into the K12 schools
is still too low.

  there is one additional wrinkle.  censorship of electronic mail is
illegal.  there are national statutes which protect user privacy in the
same manner as tampering with postal correspondence.  you need a warrant
issued by a federal court to do it.  however, no one has ever prosecuted
any school personnel for doing this, and they probably won't.   i
routinely grep the kids email as it passes through my email node on its
way to/from elementary schools.  a few times i have had to stop messages
in transit and i have an explicit mechanism set up for i document and
handle such ocurrences.  i expect that someday i may very well be called
into court over such activities, and i have prepared myself accordingly.
the advantages of educational network activity are too great to be stopped
by childish fears (or attractions) of sexual materials.

  there are other places on the networks in which you can ask for help.
back there is maasachusetts, subscribe to

                      ednet@nic.umass.edu

and you will get local help with interneting.  the issues about network
pornography and legal issues are discussed on

              comp-academic-freedom-talk@eff.org

  the moderator of this group has organized archives and files of this
material which are available by ftp.  i can supply you with more
information if you want to know about how to handle specific
circumstances.
