One of the sessions at Computers, Freedom, and Privacy '93 was about
women on the Net. One idea was women are likely to benefit from
women-only computers forums/groups. These groups allow women to
discuss to all issues (including technical ones) in a place were they
will not be "bothered" by men.

I have mixed feelings about this:

One the one hand, if the alternative is to censor the speech of
"bothersome" men in open forums, I would tend to support women-only
groups.

One the other hand, campus organizations such as the Society of Women
Engineers *don't* exclude men from their membership and they seem
to function fine.

On the other hand (!?), I believe strongly that moderated/edited
forums should be allowed to exist and that their moderator/editor
should be able to select material however he or she wants.

On the other hand, public libraries will carry material written by
Hitler, they will not however enforce rules that say "No Jews may read
this book". Likewise, a university probably could not legally help
enforce a women-only "public" forum.

On the other hand, public universities allow student organziations
that only accept some of those who apply (fraternities and sororities).

On the other hand, they don't allow student organizations that
exclude on the basis of sex (except where members live together).

On the other hand, students can belong to formal and informal
organziations that do exclude on the basis of sex.

On the other hand, these "exclusive" organziations can't be official
student organizations.

So, .... I don't know. What do you think?

Can such groups/forums be set up without violating campus rules (how?)
Why should/shouldn't a womon join such a group?

(I'm enclosing the ALA policy on meeting rooms.)

- Carl

