Well, there are some problems with this analysis.  First and foremost, there
is a big distinction between what a *student* does, and what the *university*
does.  A university can't very well control their students' associates.  (And
certainly, a public university cannot legally do so--the right to peaceably
assemble is protected by the first amendment.)

Also, fraternities and sororities are specifically exempted from federal
law prohibitions against sex-based discrimination (by recipients of federal
funding).


>So, .... I don't know. What do you think?
>
>Can such groups/forums be set up without violating campus rules (how?)
>Why should/shouldn't a womon join such a group?

Well, I won't pretend to know whether it could be done, legally, or whether
a typical university could enforce the restriction within the scope of
its own rules.

Practically, I can't help but wonder how any such rule could be enforced.
Will a new tag be attached to every account, indicating whether the owner
is male or female??

Also, it seems quite obvious to me (since Usenet is basically a publicly-
funded resource) that allowing women-only groups implies a legal obligation
to provide men-only, ethnic-group-only, neo-nazi-only, groups also (upon
request).  Otherwise, we would be dealing with clear viewpoint discrimination.

We're talking about an incredible can of worms.  My initial impulse is to
say, leave that can closed...


