I understand that a major university on the U.S. East Coast has
posted a notice that it is monitoring all usage of its computer
systems.  As I heard the story (thirdhand), the university
became concerned because of the discovery that a student
was using one of its systems to access a "kiddie porn" BBS
overseas.  (I don't know whether this has anything to do
with the recent FBI/international bust of such BBS's, but
it may.)

My question has to do with whether or not a university can
monitor e-mail usage of its systems, under current law,
simply by announcing that it is monitoring usage of its
systems.  I do not question the right of a private company
to do so under Federal law, but rather the right of
of a public organization such as a college or university,
or even a high school, for that matter.

I have a second question as well.  My sense is that the use
of USENET and the Internet to distribute newsgroups containing
erotic pictures, generally GIF files, and text erotica is about
to come under a lot of public pressure, from several angles.
We have already seen universities cutting off their feeds to
newsgroups such as alt.sex.bondage because of complaints from
some students that the content of the groups constitutes
sexual harassment of women students.  (This is, in my view
part of the general "political correctness" trend, and is of course
censorship.)  I'm not questioning the right of universities to
restrict newsfeeds, but rather I am curious about something else.

That is, is the interstate disribution via computer networks
of text erotica about, say, sex between adults and minors a fair
target for Federal prosecution?  If so, under what laws?

Mostly I am just curious about this issue, as I've been expecting
it to come under scrutiny any day now.

So...two questions about unrelated matters...

