kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) wrote:
>
>Such groups already exist as private mailing lists. Reframed, my
>questions are:
>
>1) Would it be legal for a student to maintain such a private mailing
>list on a public university machine? 

If said university allows other students to maintain their own private
mailing lists, sure; the university is merely providing a computing 
service.  As long as the university provides that service to all users
in an equitable manner, I'd think it proper.

>As part of the university's listserv services?

Hmmmm....this gets a bit dicier, since LISTSERV requires a certain
amount of support from outside personnel.

>2) If you believe that white-only country clubs are immoral,
>straight-only youth groups are immoral, and male-only civic clubs are
>immoral, could you believe that female-only private mailing lists
>are moral?

The key word here is 'private'.  If Jane Shmo wants to run a females-only 
mailing list, that's one decision; if Jane's is allowed, no one should stop
Joe Shmo from running a males-only mailing list.

If they become *PUBLIC*, i.e. LISTSERV-type "automatic subscription,
supported by University staff" lists, then you've opened the proverbial
can of worms.

If a user can run a mailing list without resources beyond those normally
allocated, I'd think we're off the hook.  We've seen the words "A site
should not be held responsible for its users' activities" hundreds of 
times in this newsgroup; shall we ignore them now?

--Wes

