>A computer/net is not much different from a library.  Both systems promote
>the exchange of information.  Both systems should be free from restrictions.
>Computer/net censorship continues because many people, unfortunately, can't 
>see the similarity between bits and books.

But they ARE different.  A library has someone in charge of "acquisition
control".  In the case of large libraries this can be a large staff (or
department).  The mix of books and periodicals are generally balanced
according to the perceived needs of the community (or the "mission" of the
school or university).  Resources (money and shelf space) are allocated
based on the goals of the library. 

I'm not a librarian, but I have a feeling if a library had a huge endowment
that allowed nearly the unlimited purchase of books and periodicals, it
would not allow people from OUTSIDE the library in another state or country
to purchase (with the libraries resources) whatever books or periodicals
they wanted and place them on the shelves.  Would a major university library
allow 10-20% of it's floor space to be devoted to adult pornography just
because someone kept putting it there?  I would guess not, yet a Usenet site
is expected to take whatever comes in off the net and serve it out - even it
it is a copyright violation (which no library I know of would condone) or
violation of the law (such as child pornography).

I'll believe a Usenet server is a "library" when you allow a similar form of 
resource control without screams of "censorship".
    
