Almost every big university netnews site must have someone doing this
task. I know the folks at my university. I believe Iowa State
University has someone doing this, since someone has to keep the list
of restricted newsgroups up-to-date.

True, this is usually one person working part-time. Why? I would think
because netnews acquisition is highly automated and usually doesn't
require a transfer of money.

>  The mix of books and periodicals are generally balanced
>according to the perceived needs of the community (or the "mission" of the
>school or university).  Resources (money and shelf space) are allocated
>based on the goals of the library. 

Making it hard for people to read things that other people don't want
them to read is usually not one of those goals.

>I'm not a librarian, but I have a feeling if a library had a huge endowment
>that allowed nearly the unlimited purchase of books and periodicals, it
>would not allow people from OUTSIDE the library in another state or country
>to purchase (with the libraries resources) whatever books or periodicals
>they wanted and place them on the shelves.
[...]

In this case, it is a user of the "library" who is requesting material,
some of which (e.g. alt.sex) is free and takes little resources.

>[...] yet a Usenet site is expected to take whatever comes in off the
>net and serve it out - even it it is a copyright violation (which no
>library I know of would condone) or violation of the law (such as
>child pornography).

These are not the reasons PSU gave when it cut alt.sex. It said that
it was being cut to save money. Since the cost of alt.sex is negliable
(and copyright violations and child porn very rare), I don't believe
these is the true reasons.

[...]
>I'll believe a Usenet server is a "library" when you allow a similar form of 
>resource control without screams of "censorship".
[...]

In a library some selection criteria are considered legitimate, others
are considered censorship. In either case, the selection criteria
should be explicit and should be applied fairly.

It seems most likely to me that alt.sex was removed because of
partisan or doctrinal disapproval. I consider this an illegitmate
reason.

