>I doubt anyone would favor such a policy.

Well, so much for the power of doubt.

Isn't Penn State a private university?  If it is, then they, as a private
organization, have the right to determine what materials they make available
to those who pay for access to them.  If you desire a change in the materials
which are made available, you can either seek another source, or try to
convince them that the change should be made.

If Penn State is a state-chartered university, however, it owes a direct
duty to the state.  The state's laws regarding sexually explicit materials
are directly relevant in that case.  The state has no right to determine
what materials are appropriate for its citizens, but a private entity has
a right to determine what services and goods it will provide.

Private censorship is not only legal, it is a fundamental right, linked
to the nebulously defined right to privacy.  If I own a book store, or run
a private library, then I have the right to determine what books my store
or library contains.  If I run a bbs with a uucp connection, then I have
the right to determine what Usenet groups I receive.  If I am a university,
then I have the right to determine many things about what happens on my
campus, including, but not limited to, the choice of books and newsgroups
that are made available by me to my students.

If you're truly opposed to what I just wrote, try arguing it with Oral Roberts
University, or Liberty University.  And then let me dictate to you how to run
any information service that you make available.

I've always been amazed at what issues students choose as their 'issues of
the day.'  Censorship seems to be among the favorite choices, and IMO it is
among the poorest.  If you are concerned about your rights to access to
information, there have to be other, more relevant places to target.  Look
at all the trouble that the CPSR has getting information from the government!
