 PC FRENZY

 taken from Campus Report, Accuracy In Academia, February 1991

  For the few months surrounding October, 1989, people were
 concerned about the homeless.  The media and Hollywood suddenly
 noticed a problem that had been around for a long time and gave it
 15 minutes of glory.  They vented a flurry of criticism about
 government policy and offered free, but superficial, advice to
 President Bush.  They even staged a big rally and concert on the
 Mall in Washington.  In the end, all they seemed to have
 accomplished was to transport a lot of homeless people to D.C.
 that they never took back, which bankrupted the city_s funds to
 deal with the homeless just before the winter months.
  Last year, it was the environment.  The same group of Hollywood
 notables, backed up by bunch of scientists who make a living
 predicting impending doom, gathered at the U.S. Capitol in April
 for a media love-fest to lament the _rape of the environment_ by
 government and big business, and to extol the virtues of the _Big
 Green_ ballot initiative in California.  Seven months later, _Big
 Green_ lost by a margin of 60-40, suffering an incredible erosion
 of public support.  Now the media are beginning to second-guess
 the environmentalists, having just discovered the results of a
 10-year, $500 million comprehensive government report that shows
 that acid rain is not the enormous problem the environmentalists
 have created.
  This year, the big domestic issue tickling the media_s fancy is
 something called _PC__political correctness.  It seems that one
 cannot open a newspaper editorial page without finding some
 columnist who seems to have just discovered this form of
 sanctioned oppression on the American college campuses.  Newsweek
 magazine even did a cover story on the trend, likening the
 enforcers of political correctness to the thought police of George
 Orwell_s 1984.
  On many campuses in the United States, the climate of free
 expression has been getting rather chilly.  A past issue of Campus
 Report mentioned that a"free wall_ for criticism of the Iraqi
 government at a Baghdad university was empty, and watched by men
 in suits who later removed the peace message that an American
 peace activist put up.  American campuses are beginning to
 resemble the situation in Baghdad, at least for those who don_t
 subscribe to the politically correct agenda.  Students deemed to
 be ignoring or in defiance of new speech codes or detrimental to
 the _community_ may find themselves thrown off campus or, worse
 yet, expelled.
  It_s a good sign that the press has finally _discovered_ the
 problems that are going on in the name of diversity and
 esteem-building.  Newsweek even roots out the source of the
 problem, writing: _If [campus radicals] no longer talk of taking
 to the streets, it is because they now are gaining access to the
 conventional weapons of campus politics: social pressure, academic
 perks (including tenure) and_when they have the administration on
 their side_outright coercion._
  This enlightenment about the evils of political correctness has
 even provoked members of the faculty, who have covertly opposed
 politically correct trends, to come out in the open.  Conservative
 and liberal faculty have found enough common ground on this issue
 to form their own support group, the National Association of
 Scholars, which has chapters springing up all over the nation.
 They_ve already become the bane of the thought police, earning the
 NAS the big three names tacked onto the enemies of the left: PC
 people say the NAS is racist, sexist, and homophobic.
  It is interesting that it has become politically correct to
 mention these disastrous trends of PC on campuses in the major
 media.  Accuracy in Academia has been doing it every month for
 over five years, but the media have never given Campus Report any
 credit.  In fact, the clear majority of the media were horrified
 by the mission of AIA when it was founded.  They decried the idea
 that someone was going to expose what professors and
 administrators were doing.  Now, however, they are fighting each
 other to cover the worst abuses.
  It is important to make sure that the media do not lose interest
 in the oppression of free speech just like they lost interest in
 the homeless and the environment.  It_s especially hard since you
 won_t see Bruce Springsteen or the cast of _The Golden Girls_
 speaking out against PC at a rally (many of the Hollywood elite
 like the concept).  In order to keep the issue alive, those who
 are under fire must be forceful in their own defense.  The media
 need to be told about what is happening, because the access to the
 media is the key.
  At the University of Texas at Austin, it was the highly effective
 letter-writing and agitation campaign by English Professor Alan
 Gribben that eventually forced it the administration to relent on
 plans to use a radical curriculum in a writing class that was
 mandatory for most incoming freshmen.  Tufts students publicly
 ridiculed their school_s speech codes, forcing the administration
 sheepishly to repeal the codes under the glare of the media_s
 cameras.
  Political correctness, like homelessness, won_t go away just
 because someone writes a story about it in the paper or holds a
 rally.  It takes a consistent and concerted effort on the part of
 those affected by it.  If those people don_t care enough to fight
 for what they believe in, the issue will die.  Let_s not have that
 happen to our campuses.  We owe it to students to let them have a
 balanced education, free of political indoctrination.
