[...]
>>It's their computer, they can do what they want. You can pay for
>>private access to the net where different levels of service are available.
[...]
>People have to stop thinking that computer services are something different
>from any other academic service.  For example, take the above sentence and
>modify it thus:
>
>"It's their library, they can do what they want.  You can pay for
>private access to the library system where different levels of service
>are available."
>
>I doubt anyone would favor such a policy.  A computer/net is not much
>different from a library.  Both systems promote the exchange of information.
>Both systems should be free from restrictions.  Computer/net censorship
>continues because many people, unfortunately, can't see the similarity
>between bits and books.

There is a big difference between "bits and books."  Books in the library
can (and I'm sure are) screened, and books not meeting certain criteria
not added to the shelves.  There are very few provisions for doing the same
for news, and certainly many ways to bypass them.

Just anyone can post to the net, and just anyone does.  Anyone can post just
about anything they want to the net, and anyone does.  Not everyone can have
a book made available at their favourite library.  For instance, name some
major universities that carry explicit, visual pornography.  How many
libraries do you know of that carry Swank magazine?  How many make hard-
core porn movies available?  How many make "kiddie-porn" available?  Not
many, I'd venture to guess.

Computer network news is a public forum, must like a club meeting or rally
would be.  It is not a library, and should not be treated as such.  Many
universities have (however ill-advised) codes restricting hatefull/hurt-
full speech in public on campus.  The same codes should be applicable to
network news articles.

