>broadening of Georgia's open meetings laws.

There are some fundamental questions here.

1) Did the court give any consideration to a possible problem with FERPA?

   The adminsitrative guidelines for interpreting FERPA clearly indicate
   that disciplinary records are covered (protected) by FERPA.  As such,
   so are judicial proceedings.  Obviously, if Georgia's open meetings
   laws conflict with federal law (FERPA), the federal law takes precedence.

This leads to the additional questions:

2) Did the court overrule the administrative guidelines for FERPA?  (If so,
   the ruling is probably meaningless unless federal courts agree.)

3) Did the court make this decision predicated on the fact that the
   hearings involved organizations (frats and sororities) rather than just
   individuals?  (In which case, they may be attempting to interpret an
   area that is ambiguous in FERPA.)


This issue has come up here at SU, with regards to publishing facts about
greek system violations.  As such, any additional information would be of
great interest to me.


