When I said a computer network was not much different from a library,
many people screamed, "But they are different!"  Then these people justified 
the removal of the alt.sex newsgroups using the same selection criteria a
library would use:
1) alt.sex is not appropriate material (a library wouldn't devote a large
   amount of space to Hustler and other pornographic magazines).
2) alt.sex takes up too much space, resources, money, etc (a library can't
   afford to buy everything: not enough room on the shelves, nor is any
   budget large enough to purchase every published item).

Ideologically, a library==a computer net==a telephone==a speaking voice.  
These are all systems for the exchange of discourses, the distribution of 
information.
In material reality, these systems differ.  Each system has some advantage
over the others.  They reach their goal through different means.  That goal, 
however, reamins the same: the impartial and unprejudiced transfer of ideas.
The final judgement on the value of any discourse rests with the individual 
reader/listener/participant and no one else.

Physically, a net is not a library.  That physcial difference, however, is
its strength.  A net is not crippled by the limitations of a library.  No
longer must we worry about a lack of space on the shelves.  Magnetic and
optical media can reduce the physical space of a library to a fraction of its
original size.  A net allows the rapid transfer of ideas.  To communicate our
thoughts to thousands of people we don't have to write a book or a newspaper
article.  We simply post to the newsgroups.  The net is a source of an
incredible amount of discourses, and it's growing every day.

What, then, is my point?

In the current world, here in 1993, computers and the net are hampered by
some of the same limitations of a library.  Computer resources are finite.  
Disk space is not unlimited.  Sadly, information must be cut at times because 
institutions truly lack the resources and funds. 
In the future, however, these limitations will fade away.  In ten years,
twenty years, sometime in the next century I will be able access through
computer all the public discourses (printed, photographic, audible, and
electronic media) ever produced--unless...
Unless people prevent the technology from being fully utilized.  In the
future, when the resources are widely available, will you people still
insist on restricting my access to the realm of human discourse?  Will you
still presumptuously judge what is appropriate for me?  I, like many people,
can think for myself, thank you.

-scott
