Scott at University of Chicago makes good points about the realtionship
between a library and a newsgroup and other forms of "expression."  What
seems most relevant in the situation at Penn State is that a public
university, essentially an arm of the government, well subsidized with
tax-dollars is choosing to eliminate some expression based on content.

Now this is not an unheard of practice by government agencies (the FCC for
example) which have the power, despite the first amendment to find matter
obscene or unfit for consumption on public airwaves.  Indeed in FCC vs.
Pacifica, it was ruled that certain material could not be broadcast during
hours when minors would be able to gain easy access to it.  Now I don't
fully agree with the ruling in the Pacifica case, but given that as
precedent, two arguments about newsgroups at PSU jump to my mind:
	
	1) PSU administration could "lock" such newsgroups to prevent
minors from access.

	2) Since subscribing to a newsgroup is more of an active form of
participation compared to radio listening, as agrued in Pacifica, is any
such regluation necessary.

In either case, I would stick to the doctrine which precludes
content-based descrimination which has been repeated by the Supreme Court
recently in the Flag Burning decision, and in the Cross Burning Decision.

Just a few thoughts.

-Alec Schwartz
